vllm bench serve: an SSE keep-alive before the first token no longer fails the request.

The request functions strip every network chunk before handing it to StreamedResponseHandler,
which splits messages on the blank line between them. Stripping removes that separator, so when
the server sends ": keep-alive" before the first token (this repo's launchers pass
--sse-keep-alive-interval 30, so any prefill longer than 30 s does), the comment is glued to every
message after it. The parser never sees a line starting "data: ", and the request is marked failed
with "Never received a valid chunk to calculate TTFT" although the server completed it. That is
why bench/run_benchmarks.sh --long read zeros for its 100k and 60k rows, and why #216 lost every
prompt of 48k tokens or more.

The chunks are now passed through unstripped (whitespace-only chunks are still skipped), and the
audio request function skips SSE comments like the other two. Checked against a local server
that sends a keep-alive before three tokens: stock fails with the error above, patched returns the
text. Upstream main has the same code.

Kind: fix
What: `vllm bench serve` no longer fails a request whose server sends an SSE keep-alive before the
  first token: the request functions stripped each network chunk, which deleted the blank line
  between SSE messages and glued the `: keep-alive` comment to every message after it. With the
  launchers' `--sse-keep-alive-interval 30`, that failed every prompt whose prefill took over 30 s
  (`run_benchmarks.sh --long` read zeros; #216 lost its 48k+ rows). The audio request function also
  skips SSE comments now
Upstream: none yet
Cut-against: 0.30.0
Retires-when: upstream PR

